# Responsive Tables

All tables must be mobile-first. Never render a wide table that overflows on 375px screens.

## Pattern

Use `.table-col-mobile-only` and `.table-col-desktop-only` to progressively show columns:

- **Mobile**: show only the essential column. Secondary data (e.g. email) is shown as sub-text inside the primary cell using `.table-col-mobile-only`
- **Tablet+ (≥ 768px)**: show full columns using `.table-col-desktop-only`

```jsx
// ✅ CORRECT
<thead>
  <tr>
    <th>Nome</th>
    <th className="table-col-desktop-only">E-mail</th>
    <th />
  </tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
  {items.map((item) => (
    <tr key={item.id}>
      <td>
        <span className="item-name">{item.name}</span>
        <span className="item-email table-col-mobile-only">{item.email}</span>
      </td>
      <td className="table-col-desktop-only">{item.email}</td>
      <td><RowMenu /></td>
    </tr>
  ))}
</tbody>

// ❌ WRONG — fixed multi-column table with no mobile handling
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Nome</th><th>E-mail</th><th>Data</th></tr></thead>
  ...
</table>
```

CSS classes `.table-col-mobile-only` and `.table-col-desktop-only` are defined in `src/index.css`.
